The original does a linear scan over every message definition on each call. This version builds a 65536-entry table, indexed by message number, the first time the function is called. After that, each lookup is a single array access.

- **Thread safety:** the table is initialized once through a function-local static, which C++ guarantees is thread-safe.
- **Data source:** it is derived only from the constant `mesgs[]` profile data, not from any mutable global state.
- **Same results:** when two entries share a number, the table keeps the earliest one, so it returns the same entry as the linear search. Numbers with no entry return `NULL` as before.
- **Fallback:** if `MESGS` were too large for the 16-bit index (not true for the real profile), the code falls back to the original linear search.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Table
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        Table()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < 65536; k++)
                idx[k] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
            {
                FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
                if (idx[n] == 0xFFFF)
                    idx[n] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
            }
        }
    };

    if (MESGS >= 0xFFFF)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
        {
            if (mesgs[i].num == num)
                return &mesgs[i];
        }
        return NULL;
    }

    static const Table table;
    FIT_UINT16 i = table.idx[num];
    if (i == 0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[i];
}
```